    "turn_text": "Yeah, I'm so glad you brought that up because this is something that I've been trying to shine a light on. Many people don't think about the fact that people who serve don't really have a choice about where they choose to serve. You're given orders. As I remember once telling my mom, mom, they're not suggestions. So if you are sent to Texas as 140,000 service members are stationed in Texas, as I was when I did flight training in Corpus Christi, Texas, you are going to have to go to the state with the 49th worst reproductive health care stats in the nation. And you're going to face everything that that implies, miscurrying in public restrooms, maybe having a miscarriage that goes so poorly that you will never be able to have kids again. maybe not having access to just basic reproductive health care. So that's why this is something I've been trying to shine a light on and trying to do just basic things through the National Defense Authorization Act. So the first thing we were successful in doing was getting through the House. We still that passed it through the Senate, but getting through the House making sure that people instead of just three months of contraception prescriptions can have a year, which is really a standard of care we're trying to move towards, and especially important for service women who might be stationed on a ship for six months. So having that year-long prescription can be so important. But beyond that, trying to just get basic stuff in place so that when a service woman changes duty stations, maybe she moves from San Diego, California to Corpus Christi, Texas, that she and really everyone in the service so that they can inform their families should have a brief on what to expect with the healthcare system. How does the healthcare system in Texas differ from what they can expect in San Diego, because you might make certain choices. What is the difference between what you can find what we say on the economy or in hospitals that are in Texas versus hospitals that are on basis? Maybe if you are having children, you want to live closer to the base if you think the reproductive health care on the base is better than what is found on the economy. So these are things that women should have access to, and these are things that doctors should be trained on. So if you are treating people in our military, you should know how to advise them, hey, you're now in Texas, you're not going to get very good reproductive health care. This is what that looks like. These are the things you have to be aware of. If you have these problems, this is how you're going to need to take care of it. And it might look very, very different from where you're coming from. If, for example, you're a good Jersey girl and you're now stationed in Texas, you should know what you're going to encounter there. And that, I have not been able to get passed through the National Defense Authorization Act, which is one of the many reasons I would argue that it is critically important that Democrats take the majority this fall as we fight for some of these just basic protections and basic information that women have a right to.",
