  {
    "episode_id": 95653,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95653.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 133,
    "turn_text": "Right, I mean, you know what we say in the military is when one person serves, the whole family serves. So when you are a service member and you have a family and you are stationed in one of these places with really horrible reproductive healthcare, and again, I think sometimes people in some of the states where it's protected don't know what's going on in this country right now, we saw the cases in Georgia where women died, one of them in a hospital, where the doctors were waiting for her heart rate and her blood pressure and her fever to get to a point where they could decide that she was at death's door so they could give her an abortion. Well, they miscalculated and she died. And so we're seeing horrible outcomes. We're seeing in Idaho women being medevacked out of the state to get reproductive health care because they're no longer offering basic necessary care there. And so when we see we have these service members who are moving around the country and who are being asked to do really difficult jobs, being asked to take their family places and then deploy for six months or then go off to a war zone with a lot of uncertainty and then to put the added burden on our families to say, look, not only is this a very dangerous job that you're doing, But we really appreciate it because you're serving your country and it's necessary for our protection. But now we're asking you to maybe put your family in danger. What if your wife gets pregnant, has a tough pregnancy, needs an abortion so that she doesn't. you know, harm her reproductive organs to the point where she'll never be able to get pregnant again. And you can't get that in your state. What if she's actually in the process of miscuing and can't get an abortion? So she develops sepsis and that's how the women in Georgia died. So she develops sepsis and can't get the abortion she needs to flush out her uterus. These are happening. These things are happening to women all over the nation. And when we're putting that extra burden on military families, it feels, it just feels as if here we're asking you to serve and we're putting not only, you know, as a service member, you know, you might put yourself in danger, but you never want to feel like you're putting your family in any sort of peril.",
