    "turn_idx": 135,
    "turn_text": "So military members and their families have tri-care. And we are one of the pieces of legislation I wanted to pass was, which we could not get behind for, was to say that EMTALA, which is what states are under, applies to military centers, meaning that you can give an abortion in the health or in a life or death situation for the mother, which that health portion is really important, saying that, look, if it's just for the health of the mother, you can do it. We wanted to make that clear for military bases, and that is not incredibly clear. Generally, it's if a mother's dying. And so it's really difficult to understand exactly what some of our military facilities can do, the VA is slightly different. And then as people are stationed overseas, they might have the military medicine, where again, reproductive healthcare is limited, sometimes still better than what you find out on the economy in some of these states, but limited, and not what we want it to be. I would like to see full reproductive healthcare on all of our military bases, including abortion, but it's limited. And then if you were stationed overseas, you can get that care, or you can, you know, it would be country by country, what care is available to you off the base and what might be supported off the base.",
