    "turn_text": "I go back and forth. I convince myself I'm not into polls and then I backslide when I see polls. So, you know, take it for when it's worth, but I feel great. I have to tell you, we are sounding like the energy on the ground here in New Jersey. We're sending busloads of people over to Pennsylvania. We're having caravans headed over to Pennsylvania. People are knocking doors. They're engaged. They're phone banking into North Carolina. They're working overtime here. I think we've seen Some just create fundraising numbers from our candidates and trying to flip some of the seats. We've got some great new members of the House and great new member, Lamanica McIver in the House of Representatives. She was just sworn in on a special. We have Nellie Poe, who I think will be the next Congressman Sue Altman's working hard on that seat in the seventh of Flip It seat. We're seeing this great energy on the ground. And I have to tell you, it's hard for me to believe that we won't flip the House of Representatives. Everyone says it's going to be tight. Some of that always depends, you know, the house is funny. It's not always based on national polling. It's often based on, you know, these smaller districts. Do you have a great candidate in the specific place? Is the Republican candidate that you're trying to beat? How good are they? How much have they broken from the party, their Republican party? Are they normal, you know, or maga? What's going on there? So, you know, a lot of this depends, district by district by district, but I just have to tell you, it's hard for me. to see the fundraising numbers in the Democratic Party versus the fundraising numbers in the Republican Party. I think that seems more telling and compelling than what the polls are saying. And",
