  {
    "episode_id": 95653,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95653.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 155,
    "turn_text": "That's a good question because New Jersey is a very democratic state. It's a very blue state, but it's not a very progressive state. I think of it as a very private state. People want to see great public school systems. They want to see good pensions for people. They want to see strong union support. They want to do great jobs and great opportunity for their kids. So it's kind of very pragmatic. It's looking at people to deliver. If you're going to serve, if you're going to be a public servant in New Jersey, then you got to deliver for the people, right? You got to break home money for the Gateway Tunnel Project, and you got to get rid of the state local tax deduction cap, and you've got to invest in our school system. You've really got to listen to people and really deliver for them. And I think that's why You know, as people are assessing candidates, I think when they see somebody, like, for example, our Senate candidate, Andy Kim, who so obviously is in politics to serve people. to serve something greater than himself, to not engage in self-dealing or to make a buck, but to actually serve the people of New Jersey. That is really compelling right now. I think especially in the aftermath of Menendez, to have somebody like that who is going to so particularly put people over self-dealing. I think that's really important to the people of New Jersey.",
